{
  "state": {
    "case_id": "111",
    "case_summary": "A man in his 40s presented with a 3-month history of discrete, dark red, circular lesions in his right axilla. He endorsed severe pruritus at these sites that would wake him up at night. He denied any recent trauma or history of similar lesions. He denied associated pain, blisters, or systemic symptoms (eg, fever, chills). His medical history was unremarkable, and he noted no environmental triggers or new medications. Treatment with over-the-counter hydrocortisone cream, ketoconazole cream, and discontinuation of underarm deodorant yielded no improvement.\n\nPhysical examination revealed 3 well-circumscribed, violaceous, annular patches without scale or erosion that are limited to his right axilla. No other lesions were noted on his skin or mucous membranes. A Wood lamp examination was negative.\n\n- Biopsy findings:  Image Title: Figure B - Hematoxylin-eosin stain  Image Description: Original magnification ×40 demonstrates lymphocytes obscuring the dermal-epidermal junction    Image Title: Figure C - Hematoxylin-eosin stain  Image Description: Original magnification ×200    - Clinical Photography:  Image Title: Figure A - Clinical photograph  Image Description: Shows oval and annular thin violaceous patches in the right axilla\n\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?\n\nOptions:\nA. Lichen planus pigmentosus\nB. Inverse lichen planus\nC. Lichenoid drug eruption\nD. Discoid lupus erythematosus\n",
    "root": {
      "label": "Subacute Unilateral Axillary Dermatological Deficit with Severe Pruritus",
      "time_course": "subacute",
      "severity": "unspecified",
      "confidence": 0.8,
      "supporting_facts": [
        "3-month history of discrete, dark red, circular lesions in right axilla",
